3. R/sysdata.rda and the two crosswalks: the Unicode emoji catalogue
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The emoji catalogue tidyEmoji detects and joins against (glyphs, their
qualification status, Unicode emoji version, CLDR short name, group and
subgroup), and the glyph and category columns of data/emoji_unicode_crosswalk.rda
and data/category_unicode_crosswalk.rda, are derived from the Unicode emoji
test data file:

Source:    emoji-test.txt, Unicode Emoji 18.0 (file dated 2026-04-30),
           https://www.unicode.org/Public/emoji/latest/emoji-test.txt

The table of standardized variation sequences the hidden-payload scanner
reads (since 0.7.0, also in R/sysdata.rda) is derived from the Unicode
Character Database:

Source:    StandardizedVariants.txt, Unicode 18.0.0 (file dated 2026-07-29),
           https://www.unicode.org/Public/18.0.0/ucd/StandardizedVariants.txt

So is the list of bases with a text presentation sequence, which detection
reads (since 0.7.0, also in R/sysdata.rda) to honour a request for the text
form of an emoji:

Source:    emoji-variation-sequences.txt, Unicode 18.0.0 (file dated
           2026-01-09),
           https://www.unicode.org/Public/18.0.0/ucd/emoji/emoji-variation-sequences.txt
So are the English keywords and names of CLDR, which since 0.7.0 extend
the keyword sets (R/sysdata.rda, .emoji_vocab):

Source:    CLDR 49.0.0, cldr-json annotations and annotationsDerived for
           English, https://github.com/unicode-org/cldr-json
Rebuilt:   data-raw/crosswalks.R in the package's source repository
Licence:   Unicode License V3, reproduced below.
           https://www.unicode.org/license.txt

The shortcode and keyword vocabulary and the detection regex come from the
`emoji` R package; see section 5.

